  "notes": "The purest key-change ballad grammar in the lane. The research pairs 73–96 with a half-time feel and the backbeat on 3, so `halfTime` is true and the snare is `halftime_3`; the 105–144 uptempo band is the straight-time wing and is named here rather than encoded. Four flat-side major tonics are given outright (A-flat, B-flat, C, F) so `keys` is authored, and the progressions are the stated I–V–vi–IV and vi–IV–I–V. The drum sentence is the model: \"programmed kit kept low in the mix — kick 1 and 3, snare/clap on 3 in the ballads, 8th shaker; a whole-bar tom-and-crash fill announces the key change\". That is the soft velocity tier, the 8th hat with almost no fill density, a shaker/tom/crash perc block, and a fill schedule pushed to every 16 bars so the tom-and-crash reads as an event. The melody is written from \"note density very low — long notes, lots of space for the string answer\": density 2–5, legato, and a counter at a low ratio doing the string answering. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it high-medium and says the tempo values are catalogue-derived; `bpm.mode` 82 is this author's centre of the stated ballad band. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are every drum probability and the bass numbers. ⚠ **`bassline.rhythm` is `boom_chick`, not `reese_sustain`** — the research names the figure outright: *“synth bass or played bass on roots, half notes in verses, 8ths from chorus 2”*. A held `reese_sustain` writes one note per chord and says the opposite of what the entry describes.",
